comments[3]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-18T11:09:40.162Z","harness:claude-code","Implemented in PR 1752. runExtensionExploreManageAction calls prepareExploreManageState(ctx, settings), which runs readAndAdoptExploreManageState (read managed state, list installed packages, adopt unmanaged ones) under withExtensionInstallLock only when manage adopts (--fix-managed-state). refreshManagedExtensionUpdates then runs after the lock is released. requiresExtensionStateLock no longer wraps manage --fix-managed-state at dispatch; doctor --fix-managed-state is unchanged. Nothing after adoption writes state: the second inventory listing, the runtime probe and triage only read. Test: tests/integration/extensions/manage-lock-scope.integration.spec.ts runs manage --fix-managed-state with a stalled npm on PATH, waits until the lookup has started, and acquires the scope lock with a single attempt, which succeeds. Negative control: moving the refresh back inside the lock makes that acquisition fail.\n"
  "2026-11-18T11:24:31.419Z","harness:claude-code","Implementation attempt on PR 1752 (67c7eef6b), reverted in the next commit. Codex review round 4 found two requirements that moving the lock alone does not meet, so this item stays open for its own change:\n1. Freshness snapshot consistency. Once the lock is released after adoption, a concurrent install can upgrade a package while its remote check runs. The second inventory scan then pairs the new manifest with update evidence computed from the old managed record, so a package just upgraded to latest can still be reported as having an update available. The fix must report one consistent snapshot, for example by re-reading managed state after the refresh and keeping update evidence only for entries whose installed identity (source, manifest_version, updated_at) is unchanged. A concurrently changed entry should report not checked rather than stale evidence.\n2. Host-error boundary. withExtensionInstallLock runs its body inside withHostEnvironmentBoundary, which turns EACCES and EMFILE from readdir into the stable, path-redacted refusal. Moving the inventory scan outside the lock also moved it outside that boundary, so it would reject with a raw Node error that carries the local path. The unlocked diagnostics need their own boundary.\nThe reverted design is still the right starting point: read state, list and adopt under the lock (readAndAdoptExploreManageState); refresh outside it; drop manage from requiresExtensionStateLock. Its test, which acquired the scope lock in one attempt while a stalled npm on PATH was running, and the negative control, which moved the refresh back under the lock and made the acquisition fail, both worked. Reuse them.\n"
  "2026-11-18T12:31:48.473Z","harness:claude-code","Mitigation shipped in PR 1752 while this item stays open: PM_EXTENSION_UPDATE_CHECK_TIMEOUT_MS is clamped to at most 25000 ms, below the 30-second lock wait (EXTENSION_INSTALL_LOCK_ATTEMPTS 120 times EXTENSION_INSTALL_LOCK_DELAY_MS 250). A single lookup batch under the manage --fix-managed-state lock therefore cannot outlast a concurrent install, uninstall or adoption. Several batches (more than four managed npm or GitHub packages, at the concurrency of 4) can still exceed it at the default budget. That is pre-existing and is what this item's structural fix, with the two requirements recorded in the previous comment, must remove.\n"
body: ""
